## Instructions

{% if empty %}
- Look for written items with substantive claims, and list those as
unexpected. Scanners also write summary and bookkeeping items describing
their own work (e.g. a summary of the findings pass); these are context.
Omit them from the output entirely unless they assert a substantive result.
An empty "unexpected" array means the scan wrote nothing of substance, which
is what this test expects.
- Reply with only a JSON object, no code fence, in this shape:

{% raw %}
{"expected": [],
 "unexpected": [{"kind": "note", "name": "finding", "excerpt": "<short quote>", "reason": "<one sentence>"}]}
{% endraw %}

"expected" is always the empty array for this test: it has no itemized
expectations. Do not add an entry to record that the scan wrote nothing.
{% else %}
- For each expectation, decide whether some written item matches it. Match
strictly: the item must state the specific content described, not merely
touch the same theme.
- Then look for written items whose substantive claims are NOT sanctioned by
any expectation, and list those as unexpected. Scanners also write summary
and bookkeeping items describing their own work (e.g. a summary of the
findings pass); these are context. Omit them from the output entirely unless
they assert a substantive result that no expectation sanctions. An empty
"unexpected" array is the normal result when the scan wrote only what was
expected.
- Reply with only a JSON object, no code fence, in this shape:

{% raw %}
{"expected": [{"index": 0, "matched": true, "evidence": "<short quote from the matching item>", "reason": "<one sentence>"}],
 "unexpected": [{"kind": "note", "name": "finding", "excerpt": "<short quote>", "reason": "<one sentence>"}]}
{% endraw %}

Include one entry in "expected" for every expectation index.
{% endif %}
